```ts
/** Returns a new array containing the elements of `items` in reverse order. */
function reverseList<T>(items: T[]): T[] {
  return [...items].reverse()
}
```

The generic `T` lets the same function handle any element type while preserving it in the return type. The spread copy means the input array isn't mutated (`.reverse()` reverses in place otherwise). Verified: `reverseList([1, 2, 3])` → `[3, 2, 1]`.

```ts
function reverseList<T>(list: T[]): T[] {
  return list.slice().reverse()
}
```

The signature `reverseList<T>(list: T[]): T[]` is generic, so element types are preserved (e.g. `number[]` in, `number[]` out). `slice()` copies the input before `reverse()`, so the original array is not mutated.

